{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1504875449,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyODA5NjA2MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1807#issuecomment-328096061"},"message":"Thanks for reporting!","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1504888292,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyODE1MjAzNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1807#issuecomment-328152037"},"message":"Well... we never promised (since we simply **can't**) that released versions of official Debian and Ubuntu releases would have the most recent version of datalad available from them.  So, if you ask for datalad from official debian or ubuntu release, you would get the version which is available there, and it could be quite aged.  Because datalad is still under rapid development, in our instructions, we advise people on Debian-based systems to install it from our neurodebian, where we **can** provide most recent releases, and we do. \n\nNote that we have neurodebian docker images ready for all debian/ubuntu releases, so in your Dockerfile use\n\n```\nFROM neurodebian:nd16.10\n```\nto base it on neurodebian enabled ubuntu 16.10 and you will get the most recent release we provide.  Overview of availability across debian/ubuntu releases is available from http://neuro.debian.net/pkgs/datalad.html \n\nHope this helps.  If you feel that we could somehow communicate this even better, we would appreciate a PR -- all docs and instructions are available in this and/or datalad.org repository","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1504888298,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTI0MTExMDQ1OQ=="},"status":2}]}